# AM01 FPGA programming (am01-fpga.service)
#
# Loads /boot/am01.bit into the Kintex-7 with openFPGALoader, over a USB-JTAG
# adapter plugged into the CM4. Nothing happens unless that file exists, so
# programming the board externally over JTAG remains an option.
#
# YOU PROBABLY DO NOT NEED THIS SERVICE. The bitstream now lives in the
# board's own SPI flash and the FPGA loads it at power-on by itself -- see
# "Writing to flash" below. This service exists for the SRAM-load case, where
# the FPGA comes up unconfigured every boot. Verified 2026-08-31: a flashed
# bitstream survives a full power cycle and reports its VERSION correctly with
# no host involvement at all.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CABLE
#
# MEASURED ON THIS BOARD, 2026-08-31, with a generic FT232H breakout wired to
# J1 (TCK-AD0, TDI-AD1, TDO-AD2, TMS-AD3, GND-GND; leave J1's 3V3 alone, it is
# a sense pin and the board has its own supply):
#
#   FPGA_OPTS="-c ft232 --freq 1000000"
#
# Two things there are not optional:
#
#   -c ft232      NOT ft232h, and not the device's USB description string.
#                 openFPGALoader's cable list spells it ft232, mapped to
#                 0x0403:6014. `openFPGALoader --list-cables | grep ft232`.
#
#   --freq 1000000
#                 The 6 MHz default is unreliable over jumper wires. Plain
#                 JTAG shifting tolerates it -- --detect works, an SRAM load
#                 completes -- but the bidirectional SPI-over-JTAG traffic a
#                 FLASH write needs does not: the JEDEC ID came back as 0xc0
#                 or 0x40 instead of 0x016018 and the erase failed with
#                 write-enable timeouts, repeatably. At 1 MHz it reads
#                 0x016018 (Spansion S25FL128L) and writes cleanly, every
#                 time. Shorten the leads before raising this.
#
# List what is actually connected with:
#
#   openFPGALoader --scan-usb
#
FPGA_OPTS="-c ft232 --freq 1000000"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WRITING TO FLASH (persistent -- what you almost certainly want)
#
# Not done by this service, because it should be a deliberate act rather than
# something that happens on every boot. Run it by hand:
#
#   systemctl stop odo-miner
#   echo -n '1-1.1:1.0' > /sys/bus/usb/drivers/ftdi_sio/unbind
#   openFPGALoader -c ft232 --freq 1000000 \
#       --fpga-part xc7k325tffg676 --unprotect-flash -f /boot/am01.bit
#
# --fpga-part is REQUIRED for a flash write: openFPGALoader loads a
# spiOverJtag helper bitstream to reach the flash chip and needs the exact
# device+package to pick the right one. Without it: "Can't program SPI flash:
# missing device-package information".
#
# --unprotect-flash is needed once the block protection bits have been set by
# a previous write ("Error: block protection is set").
#
# The unbind is because the kernel's ftdi_sio driver claims the FT232H as a
# serial port and fights libusb for it -- see
# /etc/udev/rules.d/90-ft232h-no-serial.rules. The rule handles the common
# case; do it explicitly before anything long-running, because openFPGALoader
# resets the device on open and the kernel can win the re-probe race.
#
# Then POWER CYCLE (not reboot -- a CM4 reboot leaves the FPGA configured, so
# it will still be running the old bitstream and look like the write failed).
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Check it by hand before enabling the service:
#   openFPGALoader $FPGA_OPTS /boot/am01.bit
